WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:05.259 --> 00:00:10.200
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: Thank you very much. In that
case, we move straight on to the next formal

00:00:10.200 --> 00:00:12.990
item on the agenda, which is the Chief Executive's
report.

00:00:14.340 --> 00:00:18.560
&gt;&gt;Simon Stevens: Thank you, Malcolm. First
up, I think we should all place on record

00:00:18.570 --> 00:00:22.619
our thanks to the staff of the National Health
Service and, indeed, our own staff, particularly

00:00:22.619 --> 00:00:28.640
in the Commissioning Operations directorate,
for the enormous work that went in over the

00:00:28.640 --> 00:00:35.039
Christmas and New Year period. As Richard
will report, although the NHS was very busy,

00:00:35.039 --> 00:00:41.219
generally we had a less pressurised Christmas
and New Year than last year, and there were

00:00:41.219 --> 00:00:47.050
some fundamental reasons for that. Flu rates
were lower; the flu jab worked better; there

00:00:47.050 --> 00:00:50.530
were more beds available; and the weather
was milder.

00:00:50.530 --> 00:00:56.100
However, that should not obscure the fact
that also there was much stronger operational

00:00:56.100 --> 00:01:01.199
preparedness on the part of the vast majority
of hospitals, and very strong joint working

00:01:01.199 --> 00:01:09.070
between NHS England and NHS Improvement. We
are taking a clear look at the things that

00:01:09.070 --> 00:01:14.920
generated that improvement that were within
our control over the course of the last month

00:01:14.920 --> 00:01:19.430
to six weeks, and are looking to ensure that
those lessons are learnt for next winter.

00:01:19.430 --> 00:01:25.190
One thing that I think we will need to see
further improvement on is the rates of flu

00:01:25.190 --> 00:01:31.080
vaccination, both for at-risk groups in the
population and also for NHS staff. In effect,

00:01:31.080 --> 00:01:35.090
the effective flu vaccination rate was higher
this year, if you think of that as the number

00:01:35.090 --> 00:01:42.080
of people times the effectiveness of the jab,
but the fact is that we have got more opportunity

00:01:42.080 --> 00:01:44.060
there for next year.

00:01:44.060 --> 00:01:51.010
Also, as I note in my report, I think we shared
in the joy at seeing the NHS Choir reach the

00:01:51.010 --> 00:02:02.500
number one slot over the holiday period, displacing
the True Beliebers. Thank you! Just to prove

00:02:02.500 --> 00:02:08.560
that spellcheck can be overridden on formal
reports.

00:02:08.560 --> 00:02:14.139
More importantly now, of course, we are getting
into the hard business of closing out 2015/16

00:02:14.139 --> 00:02:19.939
and ensuring that the funding and contracting
arrangements for 2016/17 are in hand. Paul

00:02:19.939 --> 00:02:28.090
will talk about the 2015/16 financial position,
not just for NHS England but for the NHS overall.

00:02:28.090 --> 00:02:34.370
In terms of 2016/17, at our last Board meeting
on 17th December we made the allocations of

00:02:34.370 --> 00:02:42.680
funding to CCGs. We have subsequently been
doing a lot of work on the incentive programmes

00:02:42.680 --> 00:02:48.650
- the so-called CQUIN - for next year, the
standard contract and the tariff consultation,

00:02:48.650 --> 00:02:55.499
which we think will be relatively uncontroversial.
With Monitor, we will be issuing that very

00:02:55.499 --> 00:02:56.439
shortly.

00:02:57.520 --> 00:03:06.840
We also have the job, again with NHS Improvement,
of jointly agreeing the performance trajectory

00:03:06.840 --> 00:03:12.590
that individual hospitals will need to deliver
during the course of next year if they are

00:03:12.590 --> 00:03:18.099
to get access to their share of the £1.8
billion that this Board allocated for sustainability

00:03:18.099 --> 00:03:21.760
funding for next year.

00:03:21.760 --> 00:03:27.169
We have issued the blessedly concise planning
guidance to the National Health Service since

00:03:27.169 --> 00:03:34.519
we met last time, and have attempted to be
very clear about the core things that we need

00:03:34.519 --> 00:03:41.930
to secure in 2016/17, but also the quite significant
gear change we want to see in terms of the

00:03:41.930 --> 00:03:46.809
type of conversations and the planning processes
which are used to think through how the Health

00:03:46.809 --> 00:03:52.700
Service puts itself on a trajectory to improve
health, redesign care and deal with financial

00:03:52.700 --> 00:03:57.019
sustainability - the triple challenge we set
out in the Forward View - between now and

00:03:57.019 --> 00:04:05.809
2020. We are envisaging two milestone points
or check-in points - April and June/July - and

00:04:05.809 --> 00:04:12.540
will be setting out more detail on that within
the next week or ten days.

00:04:12.540 --> 00:04:17.829
Between now and our next Board meeting, looking
forward into February and early March, I want

00:04:17.829 --> 00:04:25.100
to draw attention to four things that are
of significance in terms of major upcoming

00:04:25.100 --> 00:04:28.140
reviews that are likely to be published.

00:04:28.140 --> 00:04:37.750
We are highly cognisant of the pressures on
general practice. That case has been heard.

00:04:37.750 --> 00:04:42.320
The National Audit Office produced a report
on funding for general practice, making the

00:04:42.320 --> 00:04:46.350
point that, relatively speaking, it has fallen
behind other parts of the health service over

00:04:46.350 --> 00:04:52.190
a decade or more, and also showing that, since
the creation of NHS England, rather than seeing

00:04:52.190 --> 00:04:58.410
real-terms cuts each year, actually for the
last two years general practice has seen increases.

00:04:58.410 --> 00:05:01.370
So the pivot has begun, but there is a lot
more to be done.

00:05:01.370 --> 00:05:07.790
At the 17 December meeting, in allocations,
we put our thumb on the scale to ensure that

00:05:07.790 --> 00:05:13.300
general practice would see funding increases
of at least 4% a year over the next five years,

00:05:13.300 --> 00:05:18.380
but of course that is the floor rate. That
does not take account of additional resources

00:05:18.380 --> 00:05:23.440
that CCGs will be able to invest in primary
care through CCG co commissioning; it does

00:05:23.440 --> 00:05:28.720
not take account of the share of the overall
Sustainability and Transformation Fund that

00:05:28.720 --> 00:05:35.500
we would expect to see invested in expanded
primary care services over the next five years;

00:05:35.500 --> 00:05:41.390
and it does not take account of the share
of technology or primary mental healthcare

00:05:41.390 --> 00:05:43.620
funding and other funding streams that we
want to see.

00:05:43.620 --> 00:05:48.710
We are working closely with the Royal College
of GPs and the General Practitioners Committee

00:05:48.710 --> 00:05:54.490
on what a comprehensive package of support
for general practice should look like. Obviously,

00:05:54.490 --> 00:06:02.900
a small building block of that is ensuring
that the 2016/17 GP contract is reasonable

00:06:02.900 --> 00:06:10.020
to all sides, but nobody is of the view that,
by itself, that represents the sort of support

00:06:10.020 --> 00:06:13.710
that GPs will clearly need.

00:06:13.710 --> 00:06:19.930
On to mental health, under the leadership
of the independent Mental Health Taskforce.

00:06:19.930 --> 00:06:24.840
They have set out their stall on some of the
key improvements that they want to see, which

00:06:24.840 --> 00:06:31.620
we have backed, but their comprehensive report
will be published within the next several

00:06:31.620 --> 00:06:39.390
weeks. That contains a number of really important
themes, one being that if we are serious about

00:06:39.390 --> 00:06:43.500
parity of esteem or equal treatment for mental
health problems alongside physical health

00:06:43.500 --> 00:06:49.090
problems then, for all the talk of seven-day
services and crisis care on the physical health

00:06:49.090 --> 00:06:53.700
side, that same level of access should be
available at times of mental health crisis.

00:06:53.700 --> 00:06:59.420
They also make the point that, for many people
with mental health problems, their physical

00:06:59.420 --> 00:07:03.340
health needs are going unaddressed, and for
many people with long-term physical health

00:07:03.340 --> 00:07:08.240
problems their mental health needs are going
unaddressed. So a big focus of the expansion

00:07:08.240 --> 00:07:13.070
in psychological therapies will be in that
area.

00:07:13.070 --> 00:07:20.330
Thirdly, they make the point that unmet mental
health needs have big impacts on life chances

00:07:20.330 --> 00:07:26.500
and on broader wellbeing, including the economic
and inequality prospects of many people across

00:07:26.500 --> 00:07:31.540
our country. Viewing the investments we need
to make in mental health services through

00:07:31.540 --> 00:07:38.390
the life chances, inequalities and economic
benefits lens is particularly important in

00:07:38.390 --> 00:07:40.010
this area.

00:07:40.010 --> 00:07:45.550
Thirdly, the work that Julia Cumberlege and
Cyril Chantler have been leading on maternity

00:07:45.550 --> 00:07:51.800
services, particularly looking at the lessons
of the Morecambe Bay inquiry, given that Dr

00:07:51.800 --> 00:08:01.020
Bill Kirkup and Catherine Calderwood are on
the review panel, is nearing its conclusion.

00:08:01.020 --> 00:08:04.889
They expect to be able to publish that by
the end of February.

00:08:04.889 --> 00:08:13.150
Fourthly, a strategy that is not ours but
to which we are contributing: the Government's

00:08:13.150 --> 00:08:19.550
childhood obesity strategy. We have continued
to take a leadership role in terms of the

00:08:19.550 --> 00:08:25.910
contribution that the NHS can make there,
including in respect of reducing the obesogenic

00:08:25.910 --> 00:08:30.740
environment, thinking about regulatory or
fiscal measures that could be taken to get

00:08:30.740 --> 00:08:40.580
junk food out of the NHS, and better support
for patients, staff and visitors.

00:08:40.580 --> 00:08:51.680
The two final things I wanted to say were,
first, in respect of the junior doctors' dispute,

00:08:51.680 --> 00:08:56.920
to just remind the Board that under the Civil
Contingencies Act and the Health and Social

00:08:56.920 --> 00:09:03.930
Care Act, at times of industrial action or
other service disruption NHS England has statutory

00:09:03.930 --> 00:09:09.980
responsibilities to oversee the preparedness
and the response of the NHS as a whole.

00:09:10.640 --> 00:09:17.820
I want to thank Bruce and Anne Rainsberry for
the work that they did during January in respect

00:09:17.830 --> 00:09:21.500
of the strike that took place then.

00:09:21.500 --> 00:09:29.060
As things stand, there is further action planned
for 10th February, and it is fair to say that

00:09:29.060 --> 00:09:35.480
most hospital chief execs, and the relevant
people across NHS England, are concerned about

00:09:35.480 --> 00:09:42.400
the effect that further multiple days of cancelled
operations or outpatients would have, or indeed

00:09:42.400 --> 00:09:44.930
the withdrawal of emergency cover.

00:09:44.930 --> 00:09:51.710
We have welcomed the fact that Sir David Dalton,
Chief Executive of Salford hospitals, has

00:09:51.710 --> 00:09:58.370
been assigned the role of lead negotiator
on behalf of the NHS, and endorse that view

00:09:58.370 --> 00:10:05.960
that, frankly, if David is unable to get a
workable negotiated agreement that works for

00:10:05.960 --> 00:10:10.230
both sides with the BMA, then it is not obvious
that anybody else can.

00:10:10.230 --> 00:10:15.240
It is very important that both sides use the
opportunity presented by David's engagement

00:10:15.240 --> 00:10:21.589
to come to a workable solution. If that does
not happen, it is not clear that any alternative

00:10:21.589 --> 00:10:24.089
approach would generate that.

00:10:24.089 --> 00:10:32.180
Finally, I want to add our congratulations
to our own staff and our partners who were

00:10:32.180 --> 00:10:38.950
recognised in the New Year's Honours. Keith
Willett, our Director of Emergency Care; Geraldine

00:10:38.950 --> 00:10:43.160
Strathdee, our Clinical Director for Mental
Health; Stephen Groves, our Director of Emergency

00:10:43.160 --> 00:10:49.540
Preparedness, who has led a lot of the work
on Ebola last year; and, of course, Harpal

00:10:49.540 --> 00:10:54.089
Kumar and Paul Farmer for their leadership
roles in respect of our work on cancer and

00:10:54.089 --> 00:10:54.820
mental health.

00:10:56.220 --> 00:11:00.040
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: Thank you, Simon. Just on
that point, to record for the Board, I have

00:11:00.050 --> 00:11:03.130
of course written formally on behalf of the
Board to our own staff to congratulate them

00:11:03.130 --> 00:11:09.850
on that. Let me invite questions and comments,
please, on Simon's report.

00:11:09.850 --> 00:11:16.990
In which case I will take it as being received
by the Board. Thank you very much, Simon.

